explanation:
The definition builds the product CW structure with `CWComplex.mkFinite`.

**Cells.** The n-cells are `Σ pq : antidiagonal n, cell C p × cell D q`, which are exactly the pairs of a p-cell and a q-cell with p + q = n.

**Characteristic maps.** The characteristic map is the product `map p i × map q j`, composed with the isometry `(Fin n → ℝ) ≃ᵢ (Fin p → ℝ) × (Fin q → ℝ)`. Mathlib's cells are unit balls of `Fin n → ℝ` in the sup norm, and the product of two such balls is again a unit ball, so this isometry identifies the product of the factors' closed balls with the closed ball of dimension n. This is the standard construction.

**Axioms.** All the CW axioms are proved and none is weakened:
- the source of each map is the open ball;
- the maps are continuous, and so are their inverses on the right sets;
- the open cells are pairwise disjoint;
- the boundary of each cell lands in cells of lower dimension;
- the closed cells cover C ×ˢ D.

**Generality.** The finiteness assumption is needed. With the product topology, a product of two arbitrary CW complexes need not be a CW complex. It is one under extra hypotheses, for example when one factor is locally compact (Hatcher, Theorem A.6). Finiteness of both factors is the standard textbook setting in which the product is a CW complex with no further conditions. It is also what makes the weak-topology axiom automatic through `mkFinite`. Extending to one finite factor, or to a locally finite one, would need a different, harder proof of the weak-topology condition, so the definition is not too narrow in the sense of the guidelines.

**Mathlib.** Mathlib's CWComplex directory has no product construction (a search for "prod" there found nothing), so there is no counterpart to cite.
